  <h2 class="mt-12 text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-[var(--ink)]">How to calculate high school GPA</h2>
  <p class="mt-4">
    If you have wondered how to calculate high school GPA, the process is simple. Each letter grade maps to grade
    points on a 4.0 scale: A is 4.0, B is 3.0, C is 2.0, D is 1.0 and F is 0. Multiply the grade points by the credits
    for each course, add those results together, then divide by your total credits.
  </p>
  <p class="mt-4">
    For example, an A in a one-credit English class and a B in a one-credit math class give 4.0 plus 3.0, which is 7.0
    quality points. Divide by 2 credits and your GPA is 3.5. A GPA calculator with credits does this arithmetic for
    every course so you never have to do it by hand.
  </p>

  <h2 class="mt-12 text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-[var(--ink)]">Weighted vs. unweighted GPA</h2>
  <h3 class="mt-6 text-lg font-semibold text-[var(--ink)]">Unweighted GPA</h3>
  <p class="mt-2">
    An unweighted GPA calculator treats every class equally, so the maximum is 4.0 no matter how hard the course is.
    It shows your raw performance on the standard 4.0 scale.
  </p>
  <h3 class="mt-6 text-lg font-semibold text-[var(--ink)]">Weighted GPA</h3>
  <p class="mt-2">
    A weighted GPA calculator rewards difficult courses. Many schools add 0.5 for Honors and 1.0 for AP or IB, so an A
    in an AP class counts as 5.0. That is why a gpa calculator with AP classes matters: it lets you see how rigorous
    coursework raises your average above 4.0. Weighting policies differ by school, so check your handbook if you are unsure.
  </p>

